===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[English]] [[dessert]] made from a [[mixture]] of thick [[custard]], [[fruit]], [[sponge cake]], [[jelly]] and [[whipped cream]].
#: {{coordinate terms|en|tiramisu|bread pudding}}
# Anything that is of little importance or worth.
#: {{syn|en|bagatelle|minor detail|whiffle|Thesaurus:trifle}}
#* {{circa|1604}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Othello}}'', Act III, Scene{{nbsp}}3,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=othello&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: '''Trifles''' light as air / Are to the jealous confirmation strong / As proofs of holy writ.
#* '''1631''', {{w|Michael Drayton}}, ''Nimphidia the Court of Fayrie'' in ''The Battaile of Agincourt'', London: William Lee, p.{{nbsp}}168,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A73861.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Olde ''{{w|Geoffrey Chaucer|Chaucer}}'' doth of ''Topas'' tell,
#*: Mad ''{{w|François Rabelais|Rablais}}'' of ''Pantagruell'',
#*: A latter third of ''Dowsabell'',
#*: With such poore '''trifles''' playing:
#* '''1722''', {{w|Daniel Defoe}}, ''{{w|Moll Flanders|The fortunes and misfortunes of the famous Moll Flanders}}'', London, p.{{nbsp}}34,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004843555.0001.000]</sup>
#*: {{...}} when they had the Character and Honour of a Woman at their Mercy, often times made it their Jest, and at least look’d upon it as a '''Trifle''', and counted the Ruin of those, they had had their Will of, as a thing of no value.
#* '''1871''', {{w|Lewis Carroll}}, ''{{w|Through the Looking-Glass}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}4,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/12/12-h/12-h.htm]</sup>
#*: ‘And all about a rattle!’ said Alice, still hoping to make them a little ashamed of fighting for such a '''trifle'''.
## An insignificant amount of money.
##* {{circa|1597}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Henry IV, Part 1}}'', Act III, Scene{{nbsp}}3,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=henry4p1&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
##*: A '''trifle''', some eight-penny matter.
##* '''1818''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''{{w|Northanger Abbey}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}9,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/121/121-h/121-h.htm]</sup>
##*: He told her of horses which he had bought for a '''trifle''' and sold for incredible sums {{...}}
##* '''1900''', {{w|Joseph Conrad}}, ''{{w|Lord Jim}}'', Edinburgh and London: William Blackwood, Chapter{{nbsp}}30, p.{{nbsp}}311,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/lordjimtale00conrrich/page/310]</sup>
##*: What’s eighty dollars? A '''trifle'''. An insignificant sum.
##* '''1975''', {{w|Saul Bellow}}, ''{{w|Humboldt's Gift|Humboldt’s Gift}}'', New York: Avon, 1976, p.{{nbsp}}462,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/humboldt00bell]</sup>
##*: “It was bad of me then not to send the fifteen hundred dollars. I assumed it would be a '''trifle'''.”
##*: “Well, until a few months ago it was a '''trifle'''.”
# A very small amount (''of'' something).
#: {{syn|en|smidgen|Thesaurus:modicum}}
#* '''1742''', {{w|Daniel Defoe}}, ''{{w|A tour thro' the whole island of Great Britain|A tour thro’ the whole island of Great Britain}}'', London: J. Osborn ''et al.'', Volume 2, Letter II. Containing A Description of the City of London, p.{{nbsp}}90, footnote,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004843899.0001.002]</sup>
#*: This Line leaves out {{...}} ''Poplar'' and ''Black-wall'', which are indeed contiguous, a '''Trifle''' of Ground excepted, and very populous.
#* '''1868''', {{w|Louisa May Alcott}}, ''{{w|Little Women}}'', Part 1, Chapter{{nbsp}}2,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/514/514-h/514-h.htm]</sup>
#*:  There was a good deal of rustling and whispering behind the curtain, a '''trifle''' of lamp smoke, and an occasional giggle from Amy {{...}}
#* '''1932''', {{w|Graham Greene}}, ''{{w|Stamboul Strain}}'', London: Heinemann, Part 4, p.{{nbsp}}180,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.458557]</sup>
#*: “Take just a '''trifle''' of French mustard {{...}}”
# A particular kind of [[pewter]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[utensil|Utensil]]s made from this particular kind of pewter.

