===Verb===
{{en-verb|trifl}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To deal ''with'' something as if it were of little importance or worth.
#: ''You must not '''trifle''' with her affections.''
#* {{circa|1604}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Othello}}'', Act I, Scene{{nbsp}}1,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=othello&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: {{...}} Do not believe
#*: That, from the sense of all civility,
#*: I thus would play and '''trifle''' with your reverence:
#* '''1813''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''{{w|Pride and Prejudice}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}56,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/1342/1342-h/1342-h.htm]</sup>
#*: “Miss Bennet,” replied her ladyship, in an angry tone, “you ought to know, that I am not to be '''trifled''' with {{...}}”
#* '''1948''', {{w|Alan Paton}}, ''{{w|Cry, the Beloved Country}}'', Penguin, 1958, Book 2, Chapter 11, p.{{nbsp}}171,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.242743]</sup>
#*: But a Judge may not '''trifle''' with the Law because the society is defective.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To act, speak, or otherwise behave with [[jest]].
#* '''1847''', {{w|Emily Brontë}}, ''{{w|Wuthering Heights}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}27,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/768/768-h/768-h.htm]</sup>
#*: {{...}} playing and '''trifling''' are completely banished out of my mind {{...}}
#* '''1953''', {{w|Saul Bellow}}, ''{{w|The Adventures of Augie March}}'', New York: Viking, 1960, Chapter 19, p.{{nbsp}}405,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/adventuresofaugi00bell]</sup>
#*: But he was terribly roused too and bound to go on; he wasn’t just '''trifling''' but intended something.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[inconsequentially]] [[toy]] ''with'' something.
#* '''1850''', {{w|Charles Dickens}}, ''{{w|David Copperfield}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}28,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/766/766-h/766-h.htm]</sup>
#*: Mr. Micawber, leaning back in his chair, '''trifled''' with his eye-glass and cast his eyes up at the ceiling {{...}}
#* '''1965''', {{w|Muriel Spark}}, ''{{w|The Mandelbaum Gate}}'', New York: Fawcett, 1967,  Part 1, Chapter 6, p.{{nbsp}}151,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/mandelbaumgate00spar]</sup>
#*: She sat in a café, '''trifling''' with her coffee spoon.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[squander]] or [[waste]].
#* {{circa|1596}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|The Merchant of Venice}}'', Act IV, Scene{{nbsp}}1,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=merchantvenice&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: We '''trifle''' time: I pray thee, pursue sentence.
#* '''1677''', {{w|Hannah Woolley}}, ''The Compleat Servant-Maid'', London: T. Passinger, p.{{nbsp}}62,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A66839.0001.001]</sup>
#*: For an honest and sober man will rather make that woman his wife, whom he seeth employed continually about her business, than one who makes it her business to '''trifle''' away her own and others time.
#* '''1818''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''{{w|Persuasion (novel)|Persuasion}}'', Chapter{{nbsp}}6,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/105/105-h/105-h.htm]</sup>
#*: As it was, he did nothing with much zeal, but sport; and his time was otherwise '''trifled''' away, without benefit from books or anything else.
#* '''1925''', {{w|Virginia Woolf}}, ''{{w|Mrs Dalloway}}'', New York: Harcourt Brace Jovanovich, 1985, p.{{nbsp}}189,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/mrsdalloway000wool]</sup>
#*: You who have known neither sorrow nor pleasure; who have '''trifled''' your life away!
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To make a trifle of, to make [[trivial]].
#* {{circa|1605}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Macbeth}}'', Act II, Scene{{nbsp}}4,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=macbeth&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: {{...}} but this sore night
#*: Hath '''trifled''' former knowings.

